<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Donatism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th–7th centuries</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Donatists</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Donatism was a Christian schism in Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> that began in 311 over the election of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. Its followers, named after Donatus, held that clergy who had handed over scriptures during the persecution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> had lost their authority. It became the majority church in much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and, despite imperial repression and the arguments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a>, lasted for centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/donatism.jpg" alt="Donatism" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles André van Loo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augustine_and_donatists.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Donatism was a schism from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> in the Archdiocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, rooted in the Christian community of Roman Africa. During the Diocletianic Persecutions the governor was satisfied when Christians handed over their scriptures as a token repudiation of faith, and critics called those who did so traditores. The Donatists were rigorists: the church must be a church of saints, and sacraments given by traditores were invalid. In 311 Caecilian was consecrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Carthage by Felix of Aptungi, an alleged traditor. His opponents consecrated Majorinus, who was succeeded by Donatus Magnus, after whom the movement is named.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Constantine gave money to Caecilian for churches damaged in the persecution and nothing to the Donatists, which worsened the dispute. A commission appointed by Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miltiades">Miltiades</a> condemned the Donatists in 313, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-arles">Council of Arles</a> in 314 ruled against them again. In 317 Constantine issued an edict threatening death to anyone disturbing the imperial peace and another calling for confiscation of Donatist church property, but by 321 he asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> for moderation. The movement spread mainly among the indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> population and blended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> with local customs. The Circumcellions, as opponents called them, were roving groups who regarded martyrdom as the supreme virtue and sometimes used violence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a> campaigned against Donatism as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. He taught that a sacrament is valid whatever the character of the priest, and he justified force against the Donatists, though he rejected the death penalty. In 409 a decree condemned them as heretics and ordered them to surrender their churches. Donatism survived the Vandal conquest and the Byzantine reconquest, and it is unknown how long it lasted. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> the charge of Donatism was made against reformers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-wycliffe">John Wycliffe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-hus">Jan Hus</a>, who held that the moral failings of priests invalidated their offices.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Donatism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A disputed episcopal election at Carthage around 311 started the <b>Donatist</b> schism, which divided African Christians for a century and prompted Augustine of Hippo, who had studied and taught at Carthage, to develop his ideas on the church and the use of coercion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It condemned <b>Donatism</b> as a heresy and ruled that Easter should be held on the same day throughout the world.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-1">1 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The province had many bishoprics and was strongly affected by the <b>Donatist</b> controversy.</blockquote><a href="/state/byzacena">Byzacena</a></li><li><blockquote>Christianity, and the <b>Donatist</b> movement, spread widely.</blockquote><a href="/state/mauretania-caesariensis">Mauretania Caesariensis</a></li><li><blockquote>Numidia was the stronghold of the <b>Donatist</b> church and the birthplace of Augustine at Thagaste.</blockquote><a href="/state/roman-numidia">Roman Numidia</a></li></ul></section>
